    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">How Sleep Cycles Work</h2>
    <p class="mb-4">
      Human sleep follows <strong>ultradian rhythms</strong> of approximately 90 minutes per cycle.
      Each cycle progresses through light sleep, deep sleep, and REM stages. Waking at the end of a
      cycle (rather than in the middle) leads to feeling more refreshed and less groggy.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4">
      The tool below models these cycles and calculates optimal times based on your target wake time
      or bedtime, using a <strong>14-minute average sleep latency</strong> window — the time it typically
      takes to fall asleep after getting into bed.
    </p>
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3">Sleep Cycle Basics</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside mb-6 space-y-2">
      <li>A full sleep cycle lasts ~90 minutes</li>
      <li>Typical night includes 4–6 complete cycles</li>
      <li>Waking at cycle endpoints reduces sleep inertia</li>
      <li>14-minute sleep latency window accounts for falling asleep</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3">Features</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside mb-6 space-y-2">
